**Android doesn't have a built-in fake home screen.** What it has are ways to hide part of your phone: Private space on Android 15 and later, Secure Folder on Samsung Galaxy phones, guest mode and app pinning. They work well, but each has to be set up or switched on beforehand, and none of them appear when someone enters a wrong PIN. CrookCatcher's Fake home screen does that: after a wrong PIN, pattern or password it shows a home screen that looks real but opens nothing, and it takes a photo when the person taps an app.

## What Android offers for hiding your apps

### Private space (Android 15 and later)

Private space is a separate, locked area for apps you want to keep out of sight.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security & privacy > Private space`

It has its own lock. While it's locked, its apps don't appear in the app list, in recent apps, in sharing or in Settings, and you can hide the space itself. Google notes that it can't be hidden from someone who connects the phone to a computer.

### Secure Folder (Samsung Galaxy)

Samsung's version hides apps and files behind a separate passcode.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security and privacy > More security settings > Secure Folder`

To hide the folder's icon, turn off **Add Secure Folder to Apps screen**.

### Guest mode

A guest gets an empty space of their own. Your files, messages and apps aren't shared with it.

**Go to:**
`Settings > System > Multiple users`

If you don't see the option, your phone doesn't support extra users.

### App pinning

Pinning keeps the phone on one app until your PIN is entered. It's meant for handing your phone to someone for a moment.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security > Advanced > App pinning`

Then open the recent apps view, tap the app's icon and choose **Pin**.

## Where the built-in options fall short

- **You have to act first.** Guest mode and app pinning only help if you switch them on before the phone leaves your hand.
- **Your real home screen still shows.** Private space and Secure Folder hide what you put in them. Everything else, like email, photos and messages, is in plain view once the phone is unlocked.
- **Nothing reacts to a wrong PIN.** Someone guessing your code just sees "Wrong PIN" and knows to keep trying.
- **No evidence.** None of these tell you that someone tried, or who it was.

## A fake home screen after a wrong PIN

CrookCatcher's **Fake home screen** is a decoy. When someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, the lock screen is replaced by what looks like your home screen. It's built from the icons of apps that are really installed on your phone, so it looks right.

It's a picture of a home screen and nothing more. Tapping an icon opens no app. Instead, CrookCatcher can take another photo each time the person taps, so the more they poke around, the more photos you get. Your real phone stays locked behind it.

There are two situations where this helps:

- **A thief or a snoop guessing your PIN.** They think they got in and stop guessing. Meanwhile CrookCatcher has photographed them and emailed you the photo with the location.
- **Someone pressuring you to unlock your phone.** Enter a wrong code on purpose. The fake home screen appears, and it looks as if you did what they asked, while your apps and data stay locked.

